ackbone algorithms affect the performance of NECSA? We answer the above questions through the following experiments.\nFigure 8 shows the performance of abstracting state and state-action pairs. NECSA_state is the curve that we only abstract the concrete states. On the other hand, NECSA_state-action means that we abstract state-action pairs. It reveals that abstracting the state-action pairs can significantly improve performance. The reason is that abstracting state-action pairs can involve more information for modeling the policy than just focusing on states. For example, state and state-action values in DRL can be directly converted to each other, and intuitively, the scores between states and state-action pairs should also be the same. However, we found that although a set of concrete states can be located in the same grid, their corresponding state-action pairs might be divided into different grids after abstracting the state-action space. Therefore variances of the scores between states and state-action pairs are amplified. Due to such variances, abstraction with state-action pairs can improve evaluation accuracy.\nFigure 9 shows the performance under different abstraction granularities. Grid-N means we divide each dimension of state-action pairs into N equal intervals. The results show that grid numbers 8 and 10 are the best abstraction choices in Walker2d-v3 and Hopper-v3. Our evaluation is limited to 10 grids since a greater grid number causes the exponential growth of the abstract number, which is very memory-consuming. We found that more grids can not always achieve better performance. For example, in Walker2d-v3, 8-grids abstraction outperforms 10-grids. The reason is that although more grids can cluster similar concrete states more accurately, as the grid number of the abstraction becomes smaller, the scores of abstract states and patterns are less representative. We set the grid numbers to 10, 5, and 5 in Swimmer-v3, InvertedPendulum-v2, and InvertedDoublePendulum-v2, respectively.\nFigure 10 shows how the parameter \u03f5 in the equation Eq. equation 4 influences the performance. The results suggest two major conclusions: (1) \u03f5 = 0.2 and \u03f5 = 0.1 are the best for controlling\nreward shaping in Walker2d-v3 and Hopper-v3, respectively; (2) a value for \u03f5 that is greater or smaller will reduce performance. The reason is that a smaller \u03f5 reduces the impact of scores, and a greater \u03f5 will increase the weight of state evaluation and completely reform the rewarding mechanism. In addition, we set \u03f5 = 0.15 for Swimmer-v3 and \u03f5 = 0.2 for InvertedPendulum-v2 and InvertedDoublePendulum-v2.\nFigure 11 shows how the backbone algorithms affect the performance of NECSA. In this evaluation, we build NECSA on TD3 and DDPG and compare the performance with the primary algorithms. The results show that (1) algorithms can achieve better sample efficiency after incorporating with NECSA and (2) the backbone algorithms affect the performance of NECSA. For example, in Walker2d-v3 and Hopper-v3, TD3 is more sample efficient than DDPG. Such a conclusion can also be drawn after implementing NECSA, demonstrating that NECSA is scalable and effective on different backbone algorithms."
